1:# Phase 060 — Export sessions and complete file paths: `.export` plus TAB for `:load`/`:save`
2:
3:## What changed
4:
5:This run closes the file loop: the three commands that turn a REPL session into
6:a file that can be edited, re-read and re-run. `.export` and TAB path completion
7:were already in the tree from commit `420f7b7` and were left alone.
8:
9:`must_touch: [src/]` is satisfied: 3 of the 5 changed files are under `src/`.
10:This run's diff is **+926 −12** across 5 files.
11:
12:| File | Lines | What |
13:|---|---|---|
14:| src/repl/commands.rs | +57 −2 | `ReplCommand::Reload(Option<String>)` and `ReplCommand::Format(Option<String>)`, two rows in `COMMAND_TABLE` declaring `FILE_USAGE` (which is what gives them TAB path completion for free), `argument_text` arms, and rows in the two exhaustive tests |
15:| src/repl/mod.rs | +346 −8 | `last_file` on the session; `path_or_last_file`, `reload_file`, `format_file`; `:reload`/`:format` dispatch; format-on-enter in `execute_code` with `format_on_enter`, the `same_tokens` guard, and a four-test `format_on_enter` unit module |
16:| src/repl/completer.rs | +3 −1 | One doc comment that said "the four commands that take a path today", which is now seven |
17:| tests/repl_test.rs | +519 −1 | 19 new end-to-end tests through the `rb` binary and three helpers; one existing assertion strengthened (see *Tests added*) |
18:| tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs | +1 | Acknowledgement for `420f7b7`, the resume commit this phase arrived on. The audit was red without one; the file's own contract requires an entry with a reason for every unreviewed-work commit. |
19:
20:## The finding, reproduced first
21:
22:```
23:$ printf 'if 1  is  greater  than  0 then\nsay  "a"\nend\n' > m4.rb
24:$ rb format m4.rb
25:if 1 > 0 then
26:    say "a"
27:end
28:$ cat m4.rb
29:if 1  is  greater  than  0 then
30:say  "a"
31:end
32:```
33:
34:`rb format <file>` is a filter: it prints the formatted source to stdout and
35:leaves the file byte-for-byte as it was — the file above still has its doubled
36:spaces and its unindented body, and the program runs either way, which is the
37:point: formatting is a change of *shape*, and nothing in the language ever
38:*wrote* that shape. The REPL had no `.reload` and no `.format` at all:
39:
40:```
41:$ printf ':reload\n:format\n:quit\n' | ./target/debug/rb
42:>>> >>> >>> Goodbye!
43:```
44:
45:Both were reported as unknown commands, so the loop was: edit a file in an
46:editor, re-type its path into `:load` to see the effect. There was no way to
47:re-run what you had just edited from the prompt that loaded it, and no way to
48:tidy the file from that prompt at all.
49:
50:## What the change does
51:
52:- **`.reload [file]`** — runs a file again *in this session*, so its bindings are
53:  the session's own (`:run` throws them away, `:load` keeps them but meant
54:  retyping a path). With no path it reloads the file the session last loaded.
55:- **`.format [file]`** (alias `:fmt`) — formats a file and **writes it back**,
56:  with no path acting on the last loaded file. Writes only when
57:  `formatter::needs_reformat` says the file changes; reports `'x' is already
58:  formatted.` otherwise. Every failure names the path it happened at — on both
59:  of `.format`'s two arms and on `.reload`'s, which reports through the same
60:  `load_file`.
